# Application instructions for every agent

This repository is a product built on Craft Engine. The engine is a pinned
dependency; application features belong to the application. This file is the
single instruction source. CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md, QWEN.md and .cursorrules link
to it; edit this file rather than giving different tools different rules.

## The engine boundary

- A request for a new product feature authorizes application changes only.
- Never edit engine/, a vendored framework directory, the installed engine in
  site-packages, or the engine release pin/lock as part of a product feature.
- Never copy framework internals into the application or monkey-patch them to
  work around this boundary.
- Read the installed API and existing application code before implementing.
  Use configuration, providers and supported extension points.
- If an engine capability is missing, document the smallest reproducible gap
  and the required public contract. Continue independent application work.
  Framework changes require a separate, explicit owner-authorized maintenance
  task in the framework repository, with tests and a release before adoption.
- An agent must not authorize an engine change merely because its preferred
  implementation needs one. Do not bypass or modify the boundary guard.

## Where features belong

| Need | Location |
|---|---|
| Business domain, records, invariants, screens | app/modules/ |
| Capability or behavior attached to public hooks | app/plugins/ |
| Presentation of existing features | app/themes/ |
| External integration and inbound webhooks | app/connectors/ |
| Application composition and shared contract types | app/, bootstrap/, config/ |

The roots above are defaults; inspect config/extensions.py for this project.
Modules and plugins share an extension runtime but have different design
responsibilities. Themes have no providers, routes or migrations. Connectors
have no views or assets. Each domain owns its data and authorization rules.

Use extension.toml dependencies, the internal proxy for synchronous calls,
events for reactions and filters for value adjustments. Do not import another
extension's implementation. Register owned contributions through the supplied
ExtensionContext, including context.app service registrations.

## Task workflow and verification

1. Read Git status and preserve work owned by others. Record the task's starting
   commit with git rev-parse HEAD before editing. Do not replace this baseline
   with a commit the agent makes later in the task.
2. Implement the feature in application-owned paths and add behavioral tests
   appropriate to the change. Keep source code, comments and documentation in
   English; user-facing copy uses database-backed translation keys.
3. Before completion, run python tools/check_engine_changes.py --base
   <starting-commit>, the relevant application tests and craft doctor after
   changing routes, views, models or identity configuration. Resolve findings.
4. In CI/review, run the guard against the target branch's merge-base or the
   reviewed starting commit. Comparing only to the latest HEAD would miss an
   engine edit the agent already committed.

The guard detects project-local source/lock/governance changes. It is not an
OS sandbox and does not inspect installed packages outside the repository.
The owner controls a separate framework-update workflow; feature tasks never
silently become framework-update tasks.

## Data and lifecycle safety

Never wipe, drop, truncate, reset or physically delete persisted data. Use
forward-only migrations and soft deletion. Never edit applied migrations.
Deactivation/uninstallation preserves extension data. Code updates require
deactivation, forward updates and coordinated worker restart before activation.
Engine updates are separate from extension updates and from feature delivery.
